sections.body:
When you write Java, you write it in a `.java` text file, but that text is never executed directly the way a shell script or a Python file is. The `javac` compiler reads your source, checks it for syntax and type errors, and -- if it's satisfied -- writes out a `.class` file containing **bytecode**: a compact set of instructions for an abstract machine defined by the Java Virtual Machine specification. This is a genuinely separate step from execution, and it has a very concrete consequence you'll run into on day one: the compiler needs to know what to name the `.class` file it produces, and the rule is that a top-level `public` class must live in a source file with exactly that class's name plus `.java`. A file named `Main.java` containing `public class Hello { ... }` fails to compile with an error like `class Hello is public, should be declared in a file named Hello.java` -- not because the code is wrong, but because the compiler has a naming contract it enforces before it will even try.

```java
// File must be named Hello.java, not anything else,
// because the public class inside is named Hello.
public class Hello {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("Hello, Java!");
    }
}
```

A file may contain several classes, but only one of them may be `public`, and that one must match the file name. This rule exists because the JVM's classloader later finds classes by name -- it needs a predictable place to look.

examples.code:
// Hello.java
public class Hello {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("Hello, Java!");
    }
}

// In a terminal:
// $ javac Hello.java      -> produces Hello.class (bytecode), prints nothing on success
// $ java Hello             -> loads Hello.class into the JVM and runs it
//                             prints: Hello, Java!

public class ErrorTiming {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int total = 10;
        int divisor = 0;
        // int result = total / "oops";   // <- would NOT compile: incompatible types
        int result = total / divisor;      // <- compiles fine, throws ArithmeticException at RUN TIME
        System.out.println(result);
    }
}

// Adder.java
public class Adder {
    public static int add(int a, int b) {
        return a + b;
    }
}

// $ javac Adder.java
// $ javap -c Adder
// Compiled from "Adder.java"
// public class Adder {
//   public static int add(int, int);
//     Code:
//        0: iload_0
//        1: iload_1
//        2: iadd
//        3: ireturn
// }

