sections.body:
Java has exactly eight primitive types: `byte`, `short`, `int`, `long` (whole numbers of increasing size), `float` and `double` (floating-point numbers), `char` (a single UTF-16 code unit), and `boolean` (`true` or `false`). The word 'primitive' has a precise meaning here: a primitive variable's storage location holds the actual value itself, directly -- an `int` variable's slot in memory literally contains the bits representing that integer, nothing more, nothing indirect.

```java
int age = 30;
double price = 19.99;
boolean isActive = true;
char grade = 'A';
```

One detail that trips people up: local variables (declared inside a method) are **not** automatically initialized to a default value -- trying to read one before assigning it is a compile-time error ("variable might not have been initialized"), forcing you to be explicit. Instance and static fields, by contrast, *do* get automatic defaults if you don't specify one (`0` for numeric types, `false` for `boolean`, `'\u0000'` for `char`) -- a distinction that matters once you start writing classes with fields, later in this course.

examples.code:
int a = 5;
int b = a;
b = 10;
System.out.println(a);   // 5 -- b's change had zero effect on a

int[] arr1 = {1, 2, 3};
int[] arr2 = arr1;
arr2[0] = 99;
System.out.println(arr1[0]);   // 99 -- arr1 and arr2 are the SAME array in memory

String a = "hello";
String b = "hello";
String c = new String("hello");

System.out.println(a == b);        // true
System.out.println(a == c);        // false
System.out.println(a.equals(c));   // true

int total = 7;
int count = 2;

System.out.println(total / count);            // 3   -- integer division truncates
System.out.println((double) total / count);   // 3.5 -- cast promotes BEFORE dividing
System.out.println(total % count);            // 1   -- remainder after integer division

