public class CommandChecker {
    public static boolean isQuitCommand(String input) {
        String normalized = new String(input.trim());
        return normalized == "quit";
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println(isQuitCommand("quit"));       // expected true, may print false
        System.out.println(isQuitCommand("  quit  "));    // expected true, may print false
    }
}

exercises.solutionCode:
public class CommandChecker {
    public static boolean isQuitCommand(String input) {
        String normalized = new String(input.trim());
        return normalized.equals("quit");
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println(isQuitCommand("quit"));        // true
        System.out.println(isQuitCommand("  quit  "));     // true
    }
}

sections.body:
When you run `java Hello`, the JVM looks, specifically, for a method matching this exact signature inside the class you named: `public static void main(String[] args)`. Every piece of that is there for a concrete reason, not tradition.

`public` -- the JVM is calling this method from completely outside your class (it isn't one of your own objects invoking it), so the method must be accessible to outside callers; a `private` or package-private main simply cannot be found and invoked by something external to your code. `static` -- this is the crucial one, and the one beginners most often ask about: when the program starts, no object of your class has been created yet -- there is no instance to call an instance method on. `static` means the method belongs to the class itself, callable without ever constructing an object first, which is exactly the situation the JVM is in at start-up. `void` -- main does not hand a return value back to whatever called it, because 'whatever called it' is the JVM's own launcher, not ordinary Java code expecting to consume a return value; a program communicates a status back out to the operating system through a process exit code instead (covered further down), not through main's return type. `String[] args` -- a required parameter slot for command-line arguments, always an array of strings, whether or not your program actually uses any arguments at all.

```java
public class Hello {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("Hello, Java!");
    }
}
```

Get any one of these wrong -- forget `static`, capitalize `Main`, use `int` instead of `void` -- and it isn't a *syntax* error; it's a perfectly valid method that the JVM simply doesn't recognize as an entry point.

Whatever you type after the class name on the command line becomes the contents of `args`, split on whitespace, each piece always as a `String` -- regardless of what it looks like. Running `java Greet Alice 30` gives `args = {"Alice", "30"}` -- note that `"30"` is the two-character string `"30"`, not the number 30; if your program needs it as a number, you must explicitly parse it, typically with `Integer.parseInt(args[1])`, which itself can throw `NumberFormatException` if the text isn't actually a valid number.

```java
public class Greet {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        if (args.length == 0) {
            System.out.println("Hello, stranger!");
        } else {
            System.out.println("Hello, " + args[0] + "!");
        }
    }
}
```

If no arguments are supplied at all, `args` is not `null` -- it's a genuine array of length zero. Checking `args.length` before indexing into `args` is the standard defensive habit; accessing `args[0]` without checking length first, when no arguments were actually passed, throws `ArrayIndexOutOfBoundsException` at run time, since the compiler has no way to know ahead of time how many arguments a user will type on the command line.

A Java program ends in one of three ways, each with a different resulting exit code (a small integer the operating system and shell scripts can inspect to know whether the program considered itself successful). Ordinary completion -- `main` runs its last statement, or hits a bare `return;`, and there's nothing left to execute -- terminates the process with an implicit exit code of `0`, the conventional 'success' signal.

`System.exit(code)` terminates the JVM immediately, right where it's called, skipping every remaining statement -- even ones in other threads -- and reports whatever integer you pass as the process's exit code. It's a blunt tool: reach for it when a program genuinely needs to stop right now with a specific status (a command-line tool signaling failure to a calling script, say), not as a routine way to end normal execution.

```java
public class ExitDemo {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        if (args.length == 0) {
            System.out.println("no arguments supplied");
            System.exit(1);
        }
        System.out.println("this line never runs if args.length == 0");
    }
}
```

Finally, an exception that propagates all the way out of `main` without being caught anywhere terminates the program too -- the JVM prints a stack trace to the standard error stream and exits with a nonzero status (conventionally `1`), signaling failure. This is worth internalizing early: an uncaught exception isn't a special, separate 'crash' mechanism bolted onto the language -- it's just the normal consequence of nothing along the call chain choosing to handle it, all the way up to the JVM itself, which has no choice but to stop.

examples.code:
public class Greet {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        if (args.length == 0) {
            System.out.println("Hello, stranger!");
        } else {
            System.out.println("Hello, " + args[0] + "!");
        }
    }
}

// $ java Greet
//   -> Hello, stranger!
// $ java Greet Alice
//   -> Hello, Alice!

public class Broken {
    public void main(String[] args) {   // missing 'static'
        System.out.println("This will never print this way");
    }
}

// $ javac Broken.java     -> succeeds, no errors at all
// $ java Broken
// Error: Main method not found in class Broken, please define the main method as:
//    public static void main(String[] args)
// or a JavaFX application class must extend javafx.application.Application

public class ExitDemo {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("about to check arguments");
        if (args.length == 0) {
            System.out.println("no arguments supplied -- exiting with status 1");
            System.exit(1);
        }
        System.out.println("this only runs if an argument WAS supplied");
        int result = 10 / Integer.parseInt(args[0]);   // could throw ArithmeticException if args[0] is "0"
        System.out.println("result = " + result);
    }
}

